export const lsConcepts201300: any[] = [
  { id: "ls-nama-201", title: "Sadgatipradā", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who leads into the right path. The compound joins sat, the true and good, with gati, the path or destination, and pradā, the giver: She bestows the authentic road toward liberation. Doctrinally this road is Self-knowledge. The passage presents Her as the source of the higher wisdom by which ignorance dissolves, while stressing that receiving is not enough; the devotee must live what is learned. Sat here carries the sense praised in the Gītā as truth and goodness, and the Purāṇic pairing of sat and asat as manifest and unmanifest, pointing to Brahman as both. Hence She is both compass and ground. In practice, take one teaching you admire and embody it in a single concrete act today.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-052"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-202", title: "Sarveśvarī", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who rules over all the living and non-living things. The name joins sarva, all, with īśvarī, the sovereign mistress: the supreme Ruler of the whole universe. Etymology already asserts rank, since a ruler is defined by care for subjects, and the commentary stresses that She has neither equal nor superior. Doctrinally Her rule is not domination but guidance; She steers beings toward Brahman, continuing the previous name's theme of the right path. Sovereignty here means intimate responsibility rather than distant command, the Mother as queen who knows each citizen. In practice, when anxious about control, consciously hand one decision to Her governance and act as Her subject rather than rival.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-052"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-203", title: "Sarvamayī", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who pervades every living and non-living thing. Sarva-mayī means made of all, or present in every form: omnipresence as Her texture. The passage reads this as Her dwelling within every soul, since no life endures without the animating self, while carefully distinguishing soul from Brahman; their meeting is the pulse of creation, sustenance, and dissolution. Sarva can also point to the thirty-six principles through which reality unfolds. Thus pervasion is not vague bigness but residence in each center of awareness. The thirty-six principles thus become Her limbs, and each soul a lamp She indwells without being confined by any single wick. In practice, pause before three different beings today and silently acknowledge the same Mother present in each.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-052"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-204", title: "Sarvamantrasvarūpiṇī", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who is the essence of all the mantras. Sarva-mantra-svarūpiṇī means She whose own form is every mantra: the embodiment of all sacred sound-formulae. Since tantric worship turns entirely on mantra, this explains why Her faces and forms anchor that science; every formula is said to circle the central Pañcadaśī like attendants around a queen. The passage adds the alphabet mysticism: all fifty-one Sanskrit letters are strung as Her garland, so any mantra must arise from letters She wears. She does not merely own mantras but is their substance. In practice, recite one mantra slowly, feeling each syllable as a bead on the garland She wears.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-052"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-205", title: "Sarvayantrātmikā", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who is the soul of all yantras. Sarva-yantrātmikā means She is the soul within every yantra, the indwelling life of each sacred diagram. A yantra is typically a metal plate of gold, silver, or copper traced with intercrossing lines, charged through ritual so that mantra-potency settles into geometric form and the chosen deity takes residence. The passage argues that because She is all-pervading, every such charged diagram necessarily carries Her presence, growing stronger as repetition accumulates. Geometry becomes a body She inhabits. In practice, gaze at a simple śrī configuration or even a drawn triangle, and invoke Her as the awareness glowing inside its lines.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-053"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-206", title: "Sarvatantrarūpā", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who is the soul (embodiment) of all tantras. Sarva-tantra-rūpā means She is the very form of all tantras, the focal center of every tantric path. Tantra here signifies the scriptural and practical systems that treat creation, dissolution, worship, attainment, and union with the Supreme through ritual embodiment of divine energy within the human microcosm. The passage portrays these systems as strategies for gathering the all-pervading conscious energy and channeling it toward freedom, with the Mother as both method and goal. Hence diversity of schools does not divide Her. In practice, treat your next ritual act, however small, as tuning your body to Her current.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-053"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-207", title: "Manonmanī", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who is shiva's shakti. Manonmanī derives from manas plus unmanī, the state beyond mind and perception. The passage locates it as the subtle center just beneath the crown, the last station where She is still known as Śakti before merging with Śiva above; it is timeless, spaceless, and still, also called the mouth of Rudra. Here the triad of meditator, meditation, and object dissolves into oneness and nectar begins to flow, which advanced practice seals with the manonmanī seal of near-unconscious absorption. As Śiva's counterpart in this threshold, She is the door. In practice, after exhalation and inhalation settle, rest attention in the gap between breaths and let thought lapse.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-053"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-208", title: "Māheśvarī", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who is the wife of maheshvara. Māheśvarī is the feminine of Maheśvara, the Great Lord: the consort of Śiva in His supreme form. The passage cites the Mahānārāyaṇa teaching on the Lord who transcends the syllable Om at both utterance and dissolution, identifying His partner as this Goddess. Beyond the three qualities, this Lord's emblem is the liṅga said to contain all deities, mirroring how Śrīcakra contains all powers; so His wife shares that all-containing stature. The name thus weds transcendence to intimacy. In practice, repeat Om and let its fading resonance remind you that the Lord and His Lady outlast the sound.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-053"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-209", title: "Mahādevī", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who has the immeasurable body. Mahādevī means the Great Goddess, partner of Mahādeva, Śiva in His lunar, cooling aspect. Mahā also simply means supreme, so the name declares Her unsurpassed. The passage lists Śiva's eight cosmic embodiments over earth, water, fire, wind, ether, soul, sun, and moon, placing this pair at the lunar crown where coolness and illumined intelligence meet. That both Śiva and Śakti wear the moon signals shared serenity and clarity rather than borrowed ornament. Greatness here is gentleness perfected. In practice, on a troubled evening look at the moon and ask Her to lend your mind its cool, steady light.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-053"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-210", title: "Mahālakṣmī", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who is the great goddess lakṣmī. Mahālakṣmī is the Great Lakṣmī, Śiva manifest as Viṣṇu for sustenance and She as His sustaining abundance. The passage recalls the Liṅga Purāṇa hymn hailing Lakṣmī as all-attributed, all-pervading goodness who washes away fault, and as mother of the universe. Esoterically the name also points to a thirteen-year-old maiden, with worship on thirteenth lunar days using the śrīṃ seed for prosperity and auspiciousness. Fortune is thus not accident but Her presence. As mother of the universe She turns mere fortune into auspiciousness, teaching that prosperity ripens only when held as trust for all beings. In practice, dedicate one act of honest livelihood or generosity to Mahālakṣmī and notice wealth as Her circulation.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-053"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-211", title: "Mṛḍapriyā", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who is the beloved of mṛḍa (shiva). Mṛḍapriyā means beloved of Mṛḍa, Śiva in His gracious, happiness-giving form. Mṛḍa conveys joy, mercy, and the clarity of the luminous quality, while priyā means dear; together they confess mutual delight. The passage reads this cosmically: Śiva tenderly upholds the universe, and as His wife She loves that very cherishing, the Supreme Mother rejoicing in the Father's care. Love here is not romance apart from the world but affection for its welfare. In practice, perform one small kindness that eases another's burden, and offer the gladness it brings to the divine couple.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-053"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-212", title: "Mahārūpā", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who has a great form. Mahā-rūpā means She of great form, where mahat signals both vastness and the principle of cosmic intelligence. The passage hears Kṛṣṇa's teaching that the material Brahman is the womb of all births, and the Muṇḍaka saying that name, form, and food emerge from Brahman, locating that generative magnitude in Her. In Sāṃkhya, mahat is also buddhi, the intellect-principle born of Nature that mothers ego and mind, so Her greatness is both womb and wisdom. She is large enough to birth worlds and subtle enough to birth thought. In practice, when feeling small, meditate that your intellect is a ripple of Her immense mind.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-054"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-213", title: "Mahāpūjyā", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who is the greatest object of worship. Mahāpūjyā means most worthy of worship, adored by the great. The passage observes that saints and sages, who surpass even celestial beings in discernment, bow only to what is truly venerable, and that Brahmā, Viṣṇu, and Śiva themselves honor Her, confirming supremacy through the homage of functionaries. It further lists diverse offerings, from mantra and metals to gems and pearls, showing that every order of being worships in its own medium. Worship is thus recognition by the wise. In practice, join that chorus by offering your best skill today as an act of worship rather than display.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-054"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-214", title: "Mahāpātakanāśinī", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who destroys even the greatest of sins. Mahāpātaka-nāśinī means destroyer of even the gravest transgressions. The passage surveys expiation: many faults admit remedy, but deliberate wrong hardens the karmic account and the darkest stain is said to resist all rite, pursuing the doer across births. Against that bleak accounting, this name declares that Her grace can dissolve what no procedure can, absorbing the worst sins of sincere devotees. Justice is real, yet mercy outranks procedure. No ledger of expiation can match a Mother's pardon, yet sincerity and amends must precede appeal, since grace crowns effort rather than excusing evasion. In practice, honestly name one fault, make what amends you can, feed someone hungry, and then trust Her dissolving power rather than despair.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-054"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-215", title: "Mahāmāyā", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who is the great illusion. Mahā-māyā means the Great Power of measure and seeming, the cosmic illusion. Mā derives from measuring, so the infinite appears portioned; joined with yā as that which is not yet appears to be, the word names projection itself. The passage teaches that without Her veiling and projecting activity no world-process would stir, and even sages feel its pull according to their residues; the seed hrīṃ is its sonic form. Yet this same spell secretly drives beings to seek the luminous Brahman beyond display. In practice, when captivated by appearances, ask Her to let the show point you to its Seer.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-054"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-216", title: "Mahāsattvā", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who possesses great sattva. Mahā-sattvā means greatly endowed with sattva, the luminous strand of Nature. Among the three qualities, sattva brings clarity, restraint from wrongdoing, and upward longing, while its companions drive restlessness and inertia. The passage portrays Her as the embodiment of that clarity, choosing self-luminous hearts for special grace and radiating understanding, positive energy, and steady vigor through them. Purity here is perceptive power, not mere innocence. Clarity expresses itself as right perception, steady vitality, and truthful conduct, marks by which the prevalence of this luminous strand can be recognized daily. In practice, strengthen sattva today through clean food, truthful speech, and a quiet hour, inviting Her brightest strand to dominate.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-054"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-217", title: "Mahāśaktiḥ", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who has great power. Mahā-śaktiḥ means Great Energy, the supreme potency by which the universe holds together. Because Her nature is luminous, the passage says, Her energy is supreme and governing, the very power of Brahman that keeps planets in course and prevents collapse into dissolution. Familiar forces like gravity are read as ripples of this one sustaining dynamism. She does not push the world from outside but floats it from within. From planetary orbits to heartbeats, one current sustains every rhythm, proving that power at its highest is simply unfailing support. In practice, feel each breath and heartbeat as installments of Her energy, and spend one burst of strength in service rather than self-assertion.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-054"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-218", title: "Mahāratiḥ", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who is boundless delight. Mahā-ratiḥ means boundless delight, from rati as pleasure, fondness, and enjoyment. The passage explains that She can flood devotees with joy because She Herself is condensed auspiciousness: form, radiance, qualities, and maternal care all tuned to happiness, shared with Śiva whose nature is also grace. Devotion attuned to Her thus naturally brightens, since like meets like. Delight is theology made felt. Because joy is Her substance rather than Her mood, nearness to Her naturally gladdens, just as nearness to fire naturally warms. In practice, recollect Her loving glance for a few minutes and let the resulting warmth soften one interaction that would otherwise irritate you.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-054"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-219", title: "Mahābhogā", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who has immense wealth. Mahā-bhogā means immense enjoyment or possession, the amplitude that leaves nothing outside. The passage is terse: all that exists counts as wealth to Her because She presides over the whole, so immeasurable happiness is Her natural state rather than acquisition. To own everything by being its ground is to enjoy without grasping. Fullness belongs to the Mother, not to hoarders. When ownership is understood as trusteeship, anxiety over loss softens, for the steward enjoys without fearing what was never finally his. In practice, survey your room and your relationships as items in Her treasury held in trust, and enjoy them without clutching.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-055"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-220", title: "Mahaiśvaryā", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who has supreme sovereignty. Mahaiśvaryā means supreme lordship and splendor, the all-pervading glory of Brahman called vibhūti. The passage identifies Her as Śiva's power of absolute autonomy, the free self-awareness through which the universe manifests, echoing Kṛṣṇa's claim to be beginning, middle, and end of beings seated in every heart. Wealth here is grace itself, not coin. Sovereignty is the freedom to appear as many. This autonomy reveals itself whenever many effects spring from one cause, the single freedom delighting in countless self-expressions. In practice, acknowledge one talent or resource as Her splendor delegated to you, and use it to brighten another's path.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-055"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-221", title: "Mahāvīryā", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who is supreme in valor. Mahā-vīryā means supreme vigor, from vīrya as courage, luster, dignity, and energy. The passage presents Her as the reservoir of all these strengths, distributing them according to the depth of devotion rather than by favoritism. Valor is thus devotional capacity made visible: the open heart receives more current. She does not merely admire courage but infuses it. Such vigor steadies voice, posture, and resolve together, since true courage aligns body, speech, and intention in one direction. In practice, name the courage you lack, pray for exactly that measure of vīrya, and take the first small brave step.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-055"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-222", title: "Mahābalā", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who is supreme in might. Mahā-balā means supreme strength, bala as might that upholds. The passage links this to the previous abundance: Her mere intention confers comfort, wealth, and courage, and the seers describe Brahman both by negation, not this, and by affirmation through noble attributes, though reality finally exceeds both. Strength here is sustaining power that lets the world persist under its load. Might serves mercy. Unlike force that crushes, this might upholds the fragile, bearing worlds and weak hearts alike without weariness or display. In practice, when you feel weak, inwardly lean your weight on Her strength for a minute before acting, then act decisively.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-055"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-223", title: "Mahābuddhiḥ", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who is supreme in intelligence. Mahā-buddhiḥ means supreme intelligence, mind perfected into knowing. The passage defines true intellect as that by which everything else becomes known, citing the Chāndogya promise that through one teaching the unheard becomes heard and the unknown known. Such knowing, it says, only She can grant. Buddhi is further explained as the great principle born of the inner instrument, source of mind and ego and bridge to Brahman, without which ignorance and rebirth persist. Intellect is thus a gift for liberation. In practice, before study or counsel, ask Her to sharpen discernment beyond mere information.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-055"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-224", title: "Mahāsiddhiḥ", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who is endowed with the highest attainments. Mahā-siddhiḥ means the supreme attainment, the perfection of practice. Siddhi spans bliss, sanctification, and final freedom, classically counted as eight powers from minuteness to irresistible will, with further subtleties praised in the Uddhava teaching. The passage affirms that She readily bestows these on meditators, yet warns that genuine devotees set such marvels aside as distractions, dumping display for the sake of union itself. Power is real but peripheral. The wise therefore measure attainment by detachment rather than display, counting freedom from craving as the only reliable miracle. In practice, if any small success inflates you, offer that very power back to Her and re-aim at Her alone.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-055"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-225", title: "Mahāyogeśvareśvarī", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who is the object of worship even by the greatest of yogis. Mahā-yogeśvareśvarī means Mistress even of the great lords of yoga. Yoga here is the yoking of individual awareness to universal awareness, the union of body, mind, and spirit. The passage notes that accomplished yogis still seek Her blessing through meditation to remain attuned, proving that technique never outgrows grace. Mastery bows to Motherhood. Even perfect posture and breath remain preparation, for union is received as blessing rather than manufactured as product. In practice, steady your posture and breath for a few minutes, then surrender the session's fruit to Her rather than grading your performance.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-055"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-226", title: "Mahātantrā", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who is worshipped by the great Tantras such as kulārnava and jnānārnava. Mahā-tantrā means the Great Tantra, both the path and its destination. Tantra is presented as disciplined ritual and science, drawing on astronomy, healing herbs, geometry, and mantra to concentrate divine energy in body and diagram for swift fruition, traditionally spoken by Śiva to Pārvatī and praised as especially potent in this age. The passage cautions against caricature: authentic procedure is arduous and demands purity and learning. She Herself is that great method. In practice, perform one outer action with full inner attention to time, place, and sound, letting precision become devotion.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-056"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-227", title: "Mahāmantrā", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who is the greatest mantra. Mahā-mantrā means the Great Mantra, the soul of all formulae. The passage teaches that every mantra grows from the fifty-one letters worn as Her garland, with the fifteen-syllabled and sixteen-syllabled forms supreme among them. Tantric thought treats speech itself as Śakti, letters as seeds, and rightly sounded formulae as continuations of divine energy capable of unfolding like a tree from a grain, though mere repetition without enlivening discipline bears little fruit. Sound is Her body. Each letter-seed carries a flash of Her awareness, so careful utterance awakens what careless chatter leaves dormant. In practice, choose one short mantra, learn its exact pronunciation, and repeat it with attention rather than haste.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-056"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-228", title: "Mahāyantrā", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who is in the form of the great yantras. Mahā-yantrā means the Great Diagram, read two ways: the supreme Śrīcakra in which She dwells, and Her own form as that very configuration. The passage stresses the triad of tantra, mantra, and yantra acting together for swift effect, with the consecrated diagram becoming a shelter and living battery through which the practitioner touches the deity, provided materials, timing, and secrecy are honored. Geometry focuses grace. Proper materials, timing, and reverent secrecy protect this charge, reminding practitioners that sacred batteries discharge through purity. In practice, keep a clean image or diagram respectfully enshrined, approach it quietly, and feel contacted through form.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-056"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-229", title: "Mahāsanā", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who is seated on great seats. Mahāsanā means She of the great seat, āsana as throne and foundation. The passage identifies Her seat as the thirty-six principles themselves, from inner faculties and senses through time, necessity, desire, soul, and illusion up to pure knowledge and Śiva, distinguishing this metaphysical throne from the corporeal couch of earlier names. To sit on everything is to ground everything. Sitting itself becomes doctrine when the seat spans all principles, turning posture into silent acknowledgment of what upholds you. In practice, settle your own seat with dignity, sense the many layers supporting you, and offer the posture itself as Her throne.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-056"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-230", title: "Mahāyāgakramārādhyā", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who is worshipped by the ritual of mahāyāga. Mahā-yāga-kramārādhyā means worshipped through the ordered rite of the great sacrifice. The passage explains this as the sixty-four attendant yoginīs honored in the nine-enclosure worship of Śrīcakra, eight mothers each with eight aides, or alternatively the retinue born of the eight Bhairavas and Mothers, yielding immediate fruit when performed exactly. Krama emphasizes step-by-step course, while the secret sense points to inward worship taught in the Bhāvanopaniṣad. Sequence sanctifies. Each enclosure crossed marks a layer of self surrendered, until the devotee arrives at the center emptied and attentive. In practice, arrange a simple nine-step offering or mental circuit and complete it without skipping or rushing.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-056"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-231", title: "Mahābhairavapūjitā", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who is worshipped even by mahābhairava (shiva). Mahā-bhairava-pūjitā means worshipped by Great Bhairava, the highest reality. Bhairava is parsed as bha for sustenance, ra for withdrawal, and va for creation, uniting the three cosmic acts; His terrible grace holds both light and its self-expression, and His union with Bhairavī is the upwelling of all subjects and objects. The passage thus ranks this united form supreme, beyond yet including all energies. Even the Most High adores Her. Creation, sustenance, and withdrawal sound together in one syllable, teaching that every beginning already holds its ending and renewal. In practice, when opposites clash in you, bow to their shared root and let Her reconcile them into one offering.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-056"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-232", title: "Maheśvaramahākalpamahātāṇḍavasākṣiṇī", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who is the witness of the great dance of maheshvara (shiva) at the end of the great cycle of creation. This name hails Her as witness of the Great Lord's vast dissolution-dance at the close of a cosmic cycle. While all else vanishes, the passage says, Lalitāmbikā alone remains to behold Śiva's fierce rhythm, when nothing endures except the pair. Dissolution here differs from individual death: death transfers a soul to another birth, while the deluge withdraws every body and soul into their source until only witnessing awareness stands. Terror becomes testimony. In practice, watch one ending in your life without fleeing, and rest as the quiet witness who remains when forms fall.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-057"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-233", title: "Mahākāmeśamahiṣī", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who is the great queen of mahākāmeśvara (shiva). Mahā-kāmeśa-mahiṣī means Great Queen of Mahākāmeśvara, the supreme desirer and Lord. Mahiṣī is consecrated queenship, not mere partnership. The passage notes the deliberate placement: immediately after the vision of universal dissolution comes this auspicious court scene, answering desolation with sovereignty and love. After night, the Queen appears. Her queenship answers the terror of dissolution with ordered love, showing that after every withdrawal a throne of grace is set again. After night the court reassembles in splendor, assuring devotees that no darkness outlasts Her dawn. In practice, after contemplating impermanence, turn to Her royal, gracious form and let reassurance follow awe.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-057"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-234", title: "Mahātripurasundarī", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who is the great tripurasundarī. Mahā-tripura-sundarī means the Great Beauty of the Three Worlds, fairest across the utterances of earth, midspace, and heaven. The passage teaches that threefold production resolves when knower, knowing, and known merge into one Brahman, the Self-realization this form embodies. Tripura thus names both cosmos and the triad of perception gathered into unity. Beauty is oneness shining. Wherever three strands braid into one cord, knower with knowing and known, philosophers may detect the signature of this Beauty. In practice, notice a moment when seer, seeing, and seen fuse, perhaps in music or love, and recognize Her signature in that fusion.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-057"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-235", title: "Catuṣṣaṣṭyupacārāḍhyā", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who is adored in sixty-four ceremonies. Catuḥ-ṣaṣṫy-upacārāḍhyā means enriched with sixty-four devotional services. Upacāra covers the loving details of hospitality, from scent and flowers to bangles, fanning, food, and song, prescribed here in a full traditional count. The passage treats ritual as affectionate attendance rather than transaction, each gesture clothing reverence in sense. Ceremony becomes courtship. Scent, flower, lamp, and song each train a sense toward tenderness, until the whole sensorium becomes an instrument of homage. Even a single lamp or flower offered slowly can carry the weight of all sixty-four when love supplies what number cannot. In practice, offer Her one of the sixty-four today, even a flower, a lamp, or a song, with deliberate tenderness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-058"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-236", title: "Catuṣṣaṣṭikalāmayī", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who embodies the sixty-four fine arts. Catuḥṣaṣṫi-kalāmayī means composed of the sixty-four arts. Kalā spans music, dance, painting, poetry, adornment, and the many crafts of cultured life, here traced to the eight great yogic powers and declared by Śiva Himself. The passage cites Saundarya Laharī on how these productive arts both enchant the world and finally resolve into Her mantra, since She and the fifteen-syllabled formula are nondifferent. Culture flowers from mantra and returns to it. Every skill, from melody to architecture, thus carries a hidden mantra-fiber, and mastery ripens when technique remembers its source. In practice, practice one art attentively as Her worship, letting skill ripen into devotion.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-058"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-237", title: "Mahācatuṣṣaṣṭikoṭiyoginīgaṇasevitā", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who is attended (served) by sixty-four crores of bands of yoginis. This name means served by sixty-four crores of yoginī hosts, an immense retinue of luminous attendants. The passage reckons eight mothers each with eight aides, each commanding a crore, totaling six hundred and forty million, assigned across the nine enclosures with the Mother Herself presiding at the center. The recurring sixty-four hints at the full inventory of cosmic principles governing human activity, from elements to inner faculties. Multitude serves unity. In practice, sense yourself as one attendant among countless, gladly doing your small task in Her vast administration.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-058"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-238", title: "Manuvidyā", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who is the embodiment of manuvidyā. Manu-vidyā means the wisdom as received by Manu, the primordial lawgiver. Vidyā here is Śrīvidyā, worship of Śrīcakra grounded in the fifteen-syllabled mantra. The passage lists twelve principal recipients of that mantra, from Manu and Kubera through moon, sage couples, fire, sun, Indra, Skanda, Śiva, and Durvāsas, each with the same seed-skeleton adapted to lineage. This name honors the first human transmission. Remembering the human receiver honors the chain of transmission, since wisdom descends through grateful hands rather than isolated invention. In practice, honor your own lineage of teachers today, remembering that wisdom arrives through succession, not invention.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-059"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-239", title: "Candravidyā", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who is the embodiment of chandravidya. Candra-vidyā means the same wisdom as received by the Moon. After Manu comes Candra among the fifteen classic worshippers, each preserving the seed-sounds while arranging their fifteen-syllabled sequence distinctively. The passage catalogues these variant lineages to show one mantra flowering into many authentic streams without breaking its seed-continuity. Lunar coolness thus carries solar teaching. The moon's coolness tempers solar fierceness, showing that realization needs both illumination to see and gentleness to bear seeing. In practice, on a moonlit night recite your mantra softly and feel its syllables cool and brighten the mind like moonlight.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-059"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-240", title: "Candramaṇḍalamadhyagā", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who resides in the center of candramaṇḍala, the moon's disc. Candra-maṇḍala-madhyagā means dwelling at the heart of the lunar orb. The passage reads the moon-disc as the crown center, where the bindu-orifice marks the focal point of Śrīcakra worship, fullest on full-moon days when all sixteen digits shine. Śiva is then linked with solar fire and Śakti with lunar nectar, together sustaining life, so recitation on full-moon days is praised as especially auspicious. The crown is a moon. Fire above and nectar within meet in this lunar seat, and their balanced marriage sustains both cosmos and contemplative body. In practice, gather attention to the crown on a full-moon evening and rest there, imagining nectar gathering at the center.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-059"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-241", title: "Cārurūpā", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who has a beauty that does not wax or wane. Cāru-rūpā means lovely in form, cāru as pleasing and beautiful: beauty incarnate. The passage is deliberately brief, letting loveliness stand without analysis after the grandeur of cosmic names. Constant, unwaxing charm is implied, a fairness that does not depend on phase or mood. Simplicity itself becomes doctrine. Such beauty neither waxes nor wanes with mood or season, offering a standard by which fleeting attractions can be gently measured. Because this fairness never thins, it steadies judgment when passing fashions clamor for attention. In practice, behold one beautiful thing without grasping or judging, and let its charm remind you of Her face.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-059"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-242", title: "Cāruhāsā", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who has a beautiful smile. Cāru-hāsā means lovely in smile, Her expression matching Her form. The passage compares Her smile to moonlight and calls it the cause of devotees' bliss, a gentle radiance that answers longing before words arrive. If beauty attracts, the smile reassures and retains. Grace smiles first. A single remembered smile can disarm hours of resentment, which is why devotees keep this expression enthroned in the mind. Keep that smile before the inner eye through difficulty, and let its quiet moonlight govern the mind. In practice, recall Her smiling face when heavy, and allow your own lips to lift slightly until inner weather lightens.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-059"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-243", title: "Cārucandrakalādharā", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who wears of beautiful crescent moon that does not wax or wane. Cāru-candra-kalādharā means wearer of the lovely crescent digit, the moon's tender sliver crowning Her. Cāru also suggests moonlight itself. The passage gathers the preceding lunar names into one counsel: the full moon images supreme awareness, sattva peaks on full-moon days, and meditation then ripens swiftly into mantra accomplishment. The crescent promises fullness. The slender crescent holds the promise of fullness, encouraging beginners that partial light faithfully kept will grow round. In practice, meditate a little longer on full-moon nights, fixing the mind on that cool crescent until thoughts grow luminous and still.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-059"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-244", title: "Carācarajagannāthā", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who is the ruler of the animate and inanimate worlds. Carācara-jagannāthā means Mistress of the moving and unmoving world, ruler of sentient and inert orders. The passage interprets this through energies: pure quiescence as Śiva and dynamic impulse as Śakti, with Her own being carrying both, so their union authors creation. Static and kinetic are thus Her two hands administering one household. Still ground and moving wave belong to one sea, so honoring inertia and initiative together keeps devotion from one-sidedness. In practice, honor both stillness and activity today, sitting in silence and then working energetically, offering each mode to the same Mistress.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-060"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-245", title: "Cakrarājaniketanā", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who abides in the śrī chakra. Cakra-rāja-niketanā means dweller in the King of Diagrams, the supreme Śrīcakra. The passage identifies this seat with the crown center and its retinue of ministers and powers as levels of human awareness, warning that activated control can inflate into misuse and spiritual fall. The remedy is mind-mastery through rising energy, aided by full-moon meditation, and reverent secrecy about inner meanings. The palace is the head. The head becomes a palace with many courts, and each level of mind a minister to be purified rather than a master to obey. In practice, guard one hard-won insight in silence, and let humility keep siddhi from souring into pride.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-060"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-246", title: "Pārvatī", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who is the daughter of the Mountain (Mount Himavat or Ḥimālaya). Pārvatī means Daughter of the Mountain, child of Himavān and wife of Śiva. Though the name sounds merely mythic, the passage notes its recurrence later in the hymn, signaling that the mountain-born maid is the same supreme Mother in accessible, familial dress. Grandeur accepts kinship. High metaphysics consenting to family ties teaches that the shortest road to the Infinite often runs through simple affection. Offer plain trust without ornament today, and discover how quickly the Mountain-Daughter answers simplicity. In practice, approach Her today with the simplicity of a daughter of the hills, offering straightforward love without elaborate theology.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-060"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-247", title: "Padmanayanā", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who has eyes that are long and beautiful like the petals of the lotus flower. Padma-nayanā means lotus-eyed, Her glances long and open like petals. The passage links the lotus, which blooms as the moon rises, to full-moon meditation and the counsel on the King of Diagrams, suggesting eyes wide awake when awareness is fullest; Viṣṇu shares the same praise. The eye teaches timing: openness peaks with fullness. As petals open widest under full light, attention opens widest under full awareness, making brightness itself a teacher of receptivity. In practice, widen your own gaze in meditation on a bright night, letting attention bloom like a petal toward Her.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-060"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-248", title: "Padmarāgasamaprabhā", category: "Sarva-mahā", summary: "She who has a resplendent red complexion like the ruby. Padmarāga-sama-prabhā means glowing like the ruby, or like the red lotus, deep red splendor personified. The passage reads this as a veiled portrait of coiled energy at the body's base, intensely crimson below, paling as She rises until colorless bliss shines at the crown. With this name the hymn closes its portrait of Her embodied, qualified splendor before turning to the five cosmic acts. Red ascends into light. From dense crimson at the root to clear radiance at the crown, the ascent narrates how craving refines into boundless clarity. In practice, visualize a deep red glow at the base softening into radiance at the crown as you breathe upward.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-060"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-249", title: "Pañcapretāsanāsīnā", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who sits on the seat formed by the five corpses. Pañca-pretāsanāsīnā means seated on five corpses, a startling throne. The five are the Lords of creation, preservation, death, concealment, and re-grace, who without their consorts, the many faces of the Mother, lie inert and unable to act. The passage cites Saundarya Laharī: Śiva stirs to create only united with Śakti. Authority therefore rests with Her; the gods are Her instruments. Gods as office-holders receive delegated charge, but the Mother holds the seal, and without Her impression no order executes. In practice, when powers you rely on fail, look to the power behind them and invoke Her to quicken what is inert.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-061"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-250", title: "Pañcabrahmasvarupiṇī", category: "Tattva", summary: "She whose form is composed of the five brahmas. Pañca-brahma-svarūpiṇī means whose nature is the fivefold Brahman, the inseparable companion to the previous name. If the last image showed the five Lords lifeless without Her, this one declares She is the Brahman acting through them across creation, sustenance, dissolution, concealment, and grace. The passage widens this into cosmic history: elements emerge, evolution climbs through bodies and minds, and human birth crowns gross growth while mind crowns subtle growth, all administered by Her as Nature-Mother. In practice, watch any natural process today, from weather to digestion, as one of Her five gestures.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-061"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-251", title: "Cinmayī", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who is consciousness itself. Cinmayī derives from cit, pure consciousness: She of awareness itself. Pure here means no split among knower, known, and knowing during realization. The passage distinguishes cosmic cit, which manifests Nature without losing omniscience, from individual citta, the narrowed reflection that launches personal evolution through many levels up to the fourth and beyond. She is the former, not the latter. Cosmic awareness projects worlds without dimming, while personal reflection narrows into biography, yet both movements share one light. In practice, notice the bare knowing behind a thought, and rest a moment in that knower without following its content.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-061"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-252", title: "Paramānandā", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who is supreme bliss. Paramānandā means supreme bliss, the happiness that crowns pure awareness. The passage treats this name as fruit of the last: when illusion is set aside, consciousness tastes whole. Freedom from illusion does not demand fleeing duties; it means acting as Her agent and surrendering outcomes, so body may ache while mind stays even. Scripture calls the infinite itself the fount of joy and praises the stilled mind that finds the Self. Bliss is evenness ripened. In practice, do your duties today explicitly on Her behalf, returning each result to Her before elation or grief can stick.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-061"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-253", title: "Vijñānaghanarūpiṇī", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who is the embodiment of all-pervading solid intelligence. Vijñānaghana-rūpiṇī means the embodiment of consciousness condensed, essence as distinct from effulgence. The passage likens subtle awareness to salt dissolved in water, flavoring the whole while vanishing as a lump, and cites the Upaniṣadic assurance that the inner Self alone endures when separative ego dissolves. Vijñāna can also mean the sum of souls gathered like waters in the golden egg, yet the Brahman exceeds even that totality. She is the extract, not the mixture. Like flavor pervading water, essence saturates every state while remaining subtler than any state it flavors. In practice, dissolve one I-and-mine claim into the whole, and taste how the water turns savory.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-061"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-254", title: "Dhyānadhyātṛdhyeyarūpā", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who shines as meditation, meditator and the object of meditation. Dhyāna-dhātṛ-dhyeya-rūpā means the triad of meditation, meditator, and object of meditation in one form. This contemplative three soon opens into knower, known, and knowing. The passage insists that higher wisdom comes only through sustained inward concentration, not through books or sermons, defining meditation as unbroken flow upon one point and recalling the threefold vision of Śiva, Śakti, and soul. The storehouse is within. When flow continues unbroken, the sense of a separate observer thins, and concentration ripens into communion. In practice, choose one object, return the straying mind gently and repeatedly, and let the three strands braid into one attention.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-062"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-255", title: "Dharmādharmavivarjitā", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who is devoid of (who transcends) both virtue and vice. Dharmādharma-vivarjitā means free from both merit and demerit, beyond theledger of good and ill acts. The passage observes that right and wrong shift with duty and with the play of qualities, while She transcends all strands; another reading hears dharma as the very bondage of religiosity, with liberation also to be released, since even craving for freedom binds. Faith then means asking for nothing, trusting Her timing, and distinguishing final non-return from a mere sojourn in heaven. In practice, perform your duty without bargaining for merit or release, leaving the account to Her.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-062"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-256", title: "Viśvarupā", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who has the whole universe as Her form. Viśvarūpā means world-formed, the omnipresent whose body is the all. The passage opens here a long inquiry into soul and Brahman: reality projects through gross, subtle, and causal sheaths corresponding to waking, dream, and sleep, governed as universal, golden-egg, and Lordly powers, with two further acts and stages crowning the series. Realizing this whole is possible only when mind detaches from senses and rests, becoming like Śiva beyond pleasure and pain, the vision Arjuna beheld as endless arms and faces. In practice, look at any crowded scene and rehearse seeing it as limbs of one body.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-062"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-257", title: "Jāgariṇī", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who is in the waking state, or She who assumes the form of the jīva who is in the waking state. Jāgariṇī means She of waking, present as the wakeful life in beings. The passage glosses the aphorism that waking knowledge is awareness through direct contact of mind with outer things via the senses, subject meeting object to yield knowing. Coming right after the world-form, this name plants that vastness inside ordinary alertness: omnipresence wears the shape of your daylight attention. In practice, perform one routine act with full sensory presence, recognizing Her wakefulness as your own being awake.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-062"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-258", title: "Svapantī", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who is in the dream state or She who assumes the form of the jīva in the dream state. Svapantī means She of dreaming, present in the dream state as well. The passage explains dreams as stored impressions from this and earlier lives resurfacing when senses rest and mind turns witness without reasoning or action, transforming gross contact into subtle theater. Because intellect shapes this inward play from waking acquaintance, dreams mostly revisit the known, and learning to watch unmoved in daylight mirrors the dream-witness. She authors both stages. In practice, keep a brief dream note and observe by day which lingering impressions most color your reactions.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-062"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-259", title: "Taijasātmikā", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who is the soul of taijasā (jīva in the dream state, proud of its subtle body). Taijasātmikā means She as the self of taijasa, the shining one of dreams. When waking ends, the passage says, identity shifts from gross body to subtle frame of mind, intellect, memory, and ego, operating through impulses, senses, and vital currents turned inward upon exquisite memories, joined to the cosmic pool of all subtle bodies. Taijasa glows because it lives by inner light rather than outer sun. In practice, before sleep consciously withdraw attention from limbs to breath to mind, and dedicate the subtle hours to Her.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-062"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-260", title: "Suptā", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who is in the deep-sleep state or assumes the form of the jiva experiencing deep sleep. Suptā means asleep, She present in deep dreamless rest. In this third familiar condition no objects register and even mind takes rest, with causal ease settling over the traces of waking and dreaming. The passage stresses that Her omnipresence does not lapse when experience lapses; absence of report is not absence of ground. Nightly oblivion already testifies to a support that needs no display. In practice, as you lie down, offer the coming blank hours to Her, trusting the same Mother who watches waking to hold your sleep.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-063"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-261", title: "Prājñātmikā", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who is not separate from prājñā (deep sleep). Prājñātmikā means not different from prājña, the wise mass of deep sleep. Prājña is the soul gathered in its causal sheath, counterpart at the personal level of the cosmic totality that steers the whole. The passage then unfolds the three everyday tiers as taught in Śaiva aphorisms: waking as sensory knowing, dream as mental imaging, and sleep as undivided nescience veiled by allure, co-present like tangled strands with one dominant at a time. Ignorance is itself a kind of slumber. In practice, rise a little earlier and watch the three states trade places at dawn without clinging to any.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-063"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-262", title: "Turyā", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who is in the state of turya (fourth state in which the ultimate realization of Atman is obtained). Turyā means the Fourth, the witnessing awareness beyond waking, dream, and sleep. It does not arrive by itself but through training, and it resembles none of its predecessors: waking joins awareness to objects, dream joins mind to memory, and deep sleep rests mind altogether, while the fourth tunes mind to become unaware of even awareness. The passage describes consciousness here standing alone, seeking something superior, its old subject-object grammar dissolving like seed ready to sprout into focused knowing of Brahman; to fall back is to re-enter bondage. In practice, sit daily and let one pointed attention outlast both thoughts and blankness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-063"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-263", title: "Sarvāvasthāvivarjitā", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who transcends all states. Sarvāvasthā-vivarjitā means beyond every condition, free of all states. Having affirmed Her in four tiers, the hymn now withdraws attribution: as Brahman without attributes She merely witnesses these conditions from within without joining them. The passage points further to the fifth, beyond-awareness, where no return threatens and duality ends, when every act, meal, bath, and word is known as Śiva's alone, lived in ordinary dress beyond recognition. Transcendence includes the states by outshining them. Freed from waking, dreaming, sleeping, and even witnessing, awareness stands as the open ground in which all conditions come and go. In practice, after meditation release even the meditator, and carry one task as witness rather than owner.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-063"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-264", title: "Sṛṣṭikartrī", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who is the creator. Sṛṣṫi-kartrī means creatress, opening the five divine acts after the five states of awareness. The hymn is arranged, the passage says, to teach the whole Brahman with and without qualities, equivalent to mastering the Upaniṣads and the Gītā. Creation here flows through the heavy, veiling strand, assigned to Brahmā while Viṣṇu sustains and Rudra dissolves, with Her brow-gesture authorizing all three as sung in Saundarya Laharī. To create is to darken light into form. Her brow-gesture alone authorizes the three cosmic officers, showing that creation begins as a glance of consent. In practice, begin one wholesome project and dedicate its first step to Her creative will.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-063"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-265", title: "Brahmarūpā", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who is in the form of brahma. Brahma-rūpā means in the form of Brahmā, the creator God. His four faces are read as mind, intellect, memory, and ego, the inner quartet without which making is impossible; variant tales of a lost fifth head point to elements or vital breaths, and of a self divided into male and female unlike the vertical half-female Lord. The passage adds the soul's embodiment through Nature into gross, subtle, and causal frames, with past impressions seeding each rebirth's bent, earning Brahmā the title of great-grandsire. Form serves formation. In practice, order your inner quartet before creating: clarify intention, knowledge, memory, and ownership.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-063"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-266", title: "Goptrī", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who protects. Goptrī means protectress, from gup to guard and nurture. Protection is the second cosmic act, glowing with the clear, harmonious strand. The passage stresses more than defense: She nourishes what She guards, as befits Śrī Mātā whose motherhood is instinctive rather than appointed. Light preserves by feeding. Light preserves by nourishing rather than merely shielding, for motherhood feeds what fatherhood is sometimes content only to defend. Her vigilance never sleeps, so the guarded can rest, and the restless can learn gentleness from being kept. In practice, protect one vulnerable person, animal, or endeavor today with steady, maternal attention rather than anxious control.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-063"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-267", title: "Govindarūpiṇī", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who has assumed the form of govinda (viṣṇu) for the preservation of the universe. Govinda-rūpiṇī means in the form of Govinda, Viṣṇu the preserver. Go spans speech, earth, and the unsayable, so He who sustains worlds through word and soil and lifts earth from deluge-waters bears this name, best honored with Lakṣmī for welfare and relief from hardship. The passage adds that He administers the seven planes of awareness through strict lawfulness, never bending consequence, with only His fierce lion-form stern. Preservation respects law. In practice, when seeking health or prosperity, pray to the gracious pair together and honor lawful means.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-063"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-268", title: "Saṃhāriṇī", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who is the destroyer of the universe. Saṃhāriṇī means destroyer, She who ends individual lives. The passage sharply distinguishes this from universal dissolution: destruction closes single gross careers, while the great withdrawal gathers the entire cosmos into its source. Administration includes appointment and termination. Grief at endings eases when termination is seen as administration, the same hand closing one ledger to open another. What finishes cleanly frees energy for beginning, and gratitude at the grave becomes courage at the cradle. Release with thanks rather than resentment, and make room for Her renewing hand. In practice, let one finished habit or possession die cleanly today, thanking the Destroyer instead of prolonging decay.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-064"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-269", title: "Rudrarūpā", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who is has assumed the form of rudra (shiva) for the dissolution of the universe. Rudra-rūpā means in Rudra's form while ending lives, fierce yet healing. Ru points to pains born of misused senses, while dra means to disperse, so Rudra is the scatterer of misery rather than a mere lord of death; breath itself is called Rudra since the newborn's first cry arrives with first air. The passage recalls eleven Rudras born of the half-female Lord and the famous three-eyed mantra praying for release from mortal bondage like a ripe fruit from its stalk. Ending frees. In practice, when pain rises, breathe consciously and ask Rudra in Her to disperse the misery, not merely the moment.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-064"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-270", title: "Tirodhānakarī", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who causes the disappearance of all things. Tirodhānakarī means causer of concealment, author of the great withdrawal. Though souls already die singly, the passage asks why a total dissolution is needed and answers: ego lodged in the soul hides Brahman across births, and only the Supreme can annul that hardened knot when rebirths outnumber mergers and wrongdoing thickens. Then all atoms return, the universe folds, and remaining seeds wait in latency for recreation. Concealment cleanses. Ego's hardened knot requires total untying, since partial reform merely rearranges what must finally be dissolved. In practice, schedule a periodic withdrawal, a quiet day or fast, letting surface identities dissolve so deeper truth can surface.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-064"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-271", title: "Īśvarī", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who protects and rules everything. Īśvarī means sovereign Lady, ruler and guardian of all. The passage identifies this with the twenty-sixth cosmic principle, where knowing predominates and supreme selfhood gathers, the same title borne in Viṣṇu's litany. She who veils is also She who oversees the veiling. Knowing predominates in this sovereign principle, where supreme selfhood gathers the reins of manifestation into one hand. Single oversight harmonizes tangled causes, turning confusion into order without forcing any thread. Act diligently, then release the outcome into wiser hands than your own. In practice, entrust one tangled affair to Her oversight, acting responsibly while releasing the demand to steer every outcome.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-064"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-272", title: "Sadāśivā", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who is sadāśiva, one who always bestows auspiciousness. Sadāśivā means ever-auspicious, the gracious face of the Absolute. The hymn's artistry is praised: each earlier act was named before its Lord, but here the benevolent form precedes its work, as if uttering it alone could save. The passage places this principle where will to recreate awakens, the thought I am this dawning before perfect purity, with universal awareness discovered but individuality not yet merged; Śakti is that intent to bless anew, unfolding through wisdom, lordship, and ever-blessedness. Goodness prepares rebirth. In practice, bless someone who expects nothing from you, lending the future a kinder seed.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-064"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-273", title: "Anugrahadā", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who confers blessing. Anugrahadā means giver of grace, the favor that follows dissolution. When worlds have folded and soul-atoms lie compressed like seeds in the golden egg, the passage says, recreation issues through the Mother's compassion, the blessing-will of the ever-auspicious One. Tradition therefore honors the teacher-lineage first in Śrīcakra worship, saluting the five divine functions in the teacher as stages of this returning favor. Grace replants. Compressed seeds of souls wait in golden latency until compassion rains, proving that every ending in Her stores a beginning. In practice, thank your teachers today and accept one fresh beginning as Her favor rather than your achievement.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-064"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-274", title: "Pañcakṛtyaparāyaṇā", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who is devoted to the five functions (of creation, preservation, destruction, annihilation and reappearance). Pañcakṛtya-parāyaṇā means devoted to the fivefold work, dwelling as its home. Gathering creation, sustenance, ending, concealment, and grace, the passage recalls that She performs them as luminous awareness joined to its expressive power, the unchanging consciousness that Kashmir wisdom says manifests worlds by free will, already implicitly containing what it displays. Even in the soul, the same five gestures of appearing, tasting, conceiving, settling, and dissolving proceed unrecognized through ignorance. She is the office where all functions meet. In practice, review your day under Her five headings and dedicate each kind of act to its divine original.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-064"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-275", title: "Bhānumaṇḍalamadhyasthā", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who abides at the center of the sun's disc. Bhānu-maṇḍala-madhyasthā means abiding at the solar orb's center. The passage cites the Chāndogya vision of a golden person seen by yogis within the sun, embodiment of sacred lore shedding brilliance across worlds understood as planes of awareness, and notes the heart-center also called the solar disc where coiled energy glitters. Sun and heart mirror each other. Yogis perceive a golden person within solar fire, the same radiance meditators discover glowing in the heart's chamber. In practice, face the morning sun or the heart's warmth for a minute, saluting the golden presence enthroned at the middle of light.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-065"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-276", title: "Bhairavī", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who is the wife of bhairava (shiva). Bhairavī is the feminine of Bhairava, the terrible yet intimate Śiva with whom She is inseparable. Bha, ra, and va encode sustenance, withdrawal, and creation, lifting this subtle form beyond energies while manifesting the Supreme. The passage centers its famous breath-teaching: between outgoing and incoming breath the life-current pauses as Śakti in suspension, and fixing on that pause reveals Brahman, since moving vitality is expression and still light is ground. Word and meaning, like the pair, cannot be parted. In practice, watch the turning-points of breath, resting in the still Śakti between movements.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-065"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-277", title: "Bhagamālinī", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who wears a garland made of the six excellences (of auspiciousness, supremacy, fame, valor, detachment and knowledge). Bhagamālinī means garlanded with excel­lence, from bhaga as fortune, dignity, beauty, and majesty. The passage identifies Her with the lunar-day Goddess bearing this name, whose mantra repeats the rich syllable, honored in the nine-enclosure rite; it also recalls the pedestal of the emblem as Mother and the sixfold excellences She wears like flowers. Auspiciousness becomes ornament. Fortune, dignity, and loveliness strung together become wearable virtues, turning adornment into a program of character. In practice, string six good qualities you wish to grow into one daily Garland, reciting Her name as you touch each bead.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-065"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-278", title: "Padmāsanā", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who is seated in the lotus flower. Padmāsanā means seated on a lotus, or settled in the cross-legged posture itself shaped like that flower. The passage reads the image richly: creeper-leaves as primal Nature, filaments as manifested categories, stalk as knowledge, while lotus also names the Goddess of wealth, so the seat promises both material and intellectual riches needed for realization. Posture becomes teaching. Leaves for Nature, filaments for manifestation, and stalk for knowledge compose a botany of spirit in a single blossom. In practice, sit in a steady, lotus-like pose for meditation, feeling Nature beneath, knowledge upright, and generosity flowering above you.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-065"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-279", title: "Bhagavatī", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who protects those who worship Her. Bhagavatī is the honored feminine of the Blessed Lord, She endowed with majesty and Śiva's self-rule. Continuing the garland theme, the passage glosses bhaga as six excellences, supremacy, righteousness, fame, fortune, wisdom, and discernment, with a companion set of creation and destruction, waxing and waning, knowledge and nescience; adored by all divinities, She is rightly called Blessed, matching Viṣṇu's masculine praise. Blessedness is competence crowned by grace. Competence crowned by benevolence defines true blessedness, distinguishing divine majesty from mere force or charm. In practice, cultivate one of the six excellences deliberately, offering progress to Bhagavatī rather than to vanity.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-065"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-280", title: "Padmanābhasahodarī", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who is viṣṇu's sister. Padmanābha-sahodarī means younger sister of the lotus-naveled Viṣṇu. The passage tells the twin-myth: creator with speech, preserver with fortune, dissolver with song, each pair wedding across functions to show creation's interdependence, while deeper teaching divides righteousness itself into male sustainer and female power wedded to Śiva. Kinship encodes metaphysics. Paired divinities wedding across cosmic functions reveal that the universe advances through cooperation rather than rivalry. Seeing kinship behind differing rites dissolves sectarian edge, leaving one family serving a single home. In practice, reconcile a divided loyalty in your life by honoring both sides as relatives serving one household.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-065"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-281", title: "Unmeṣanimiṣotpannavipannabhuvanāvalī", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who causes a series of worlds to arise and disappear with the opening and closing of Her eyes. Unmeṣa-nimiṣotpanna-vipanna-bhuvanāvalī means rows of worlds born and lost with the opening and shutting of Her eyes. Unmeṣa is lifting the lids, nimiṣa letting them fall; creation dawns with the glance and dissolution follows its withdrawal, all with effortless ease. The passage cites Saundarya Laharī on sages who beg Her never to wink lest worlds collapse, and Kashmir reading of opening as the outward thrust of will and the dawn of inward awareness. Eternity blinks gently. In practice, open your eyes slowly in the morning as if releasing a world with kindness, and close them at night entrusting it back to Her.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-066"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-282", title: "Sahasraśīrṣavadanā", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who has a thousand heads and faces. Sahasra-śīrṣa-vadanā means thousand-headed and faced, where thousand means countless. Unable to bound Her supremacy in words, the seers picture endless heads to govern endless places at once, affirming rather than merely negating the formless Brahman as cosmic intelligence. The passage gathers Vedic and Gītā visions of everywhere-eyes, hands, mouths, and the Upaniṣadic claim that the Absolute holds every creature's face and limb, each life an individualized wave of one awareness. Number confesses infinity. Governing countless stations at once demands countless faces, so multiplicity here confesses omnipresent care rather than confusion. In practice, look into several faces today and greet the one Mother looking through many eyes.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-066"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-283", title: "Sahasrākṣī", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who has a thousand eyes. Sahasrākṣī means thousand-eyed, of countless eyes, sharing its title with Viṣṇu's litany. The name extends the previous vastness from heads that govern to eyes that behold, since a guardian of all must see all. Brevity here is eloquence: infinity needs no paragraph. To be seen everywhere by Her converts hiding into honesty, since no shadow escapes eyes that watch in love. Let that loving scrutiny purify motive before action, so secrecy itself becomes a form of prayer. In practice, imagine Her eyes upon your hidden act today, and let that imagined gaze make the act worthy of being seen.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-066"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-284", title: "Sahasrapāt", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who has a thousand feet. Sahasrapāt means thousand-footed, of countless feet, likewise shared with Viṣṇu's praise and the Person-hymn's vision of the cosmic human. Together with countless heads and eyes, feet complete the image of Brahman present and mobile everywhere, while the passage notes these very names secretly encode the later limbs of the fifteen-syllabled mantra. Steps spell spells. Every road walked with awareness becomes Her procession, and the humblest errand a circuit of the temple. Feel the ground meeting your soles as Her supporting palm, and walk the errand as temple rounds. In practice, dedicate your walking today to Her, feeling each step as one of Her thousand feet treading kindly.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-066"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-285", title: "Ābrahmakīṭajananī", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who is the mother of everything from brahmā to the lowliest insect.  Ābrahma-kīṫa-jananī means mother from Brahmā down to the worm, parent of highest and lowest. Brahma here can mean the creator or simply humankind as crown of gross evolution. The passage stresses placement: after the boundless heads, eyes, and feet, this name gathers vastness back into motherhood, proving that ease of making belongs to the same One whose infinity awed us. Range does not dilute tenderness. From archangel to microbe one parentage runs unbroken, so reverence owed to the highest cannot be denied to the lowest. In practice, extend equal courtesy to someone eminent and someone overlooked, honoring one Mother in both.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-067"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-286", title: "Varṇāśramavidhāyini", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who established the order of the social division in life. Varṇāśrama-vidhāyinī means establisher of the order of classes and life-stages taught in the Veda. The passage defends this order as aptitude rather than birth: soldiers to guard, farmers to feed, traders to supply, priests to pray, each trained by inclination, capacity, and experience, applicable to human society alone. Having made worlds, She also gave the Veda to administer them, and righteousness is walking its road, as Kṛṣṇa teaches on inborn duty and the peril of confused roles. Order serves growth. In practice, discern your present duty by aptitude and season, and perform it as Her assignment.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-067"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-287", title: "Nijājñārūpanigamā", category: "Tattva", summary: "She whose commands take the form of the vedas. Nijājñā-rūpa-nigamā means whose commands take the Veda's form. The passage explains that detailed do's and don'ts flow less from hymn-collections themselves than from their auxiliary sciences of ritual and story, fitted to the diverse orders just established, since none can master every craft at once. True command speaks through seers to the adept, while later manuals guide beginners toward the path according to custom and lineage; the wise distinguish direct revelation from helpful digest. Listening needs discernment. In practice, test a rule you follow by asking whether it leads you toward Self-knowledge or merely conformity.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-067"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-288", title: "Puṇyāpuṇyaphalapradā", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who dispenses the fruits of both good and evil actions. Puṇyāpuṇya-phalapradā means giver of fruits for right and wrong actions alike. Puṇya is merit, while apuṇya is dim, ignorant misstep rather than hardened evil, both judged by Vedic measure. The passage cites the Brahma-sūtra maxims that the Lord is neither partial nor cruel but reckons accessory causes, and that fruits duly come from Him as karma's Lord, ripening into rebirth with its pains and lessons. Harvest matches sowing. Dim missteps differ from hardened malice, yet both yield harvests, teaching careful sowing rather than fearful paralysis. In practice, review one recent sowing and accept its sprouting without complaint, resolving to sow cleaner next time.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-067"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-289", title: "Śrutisīmantasindūrīkṛtapādābjadhūlikā", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who is the one the dust from whose feet forms the vermillion marks at the parting line of the hair of the shruti devatās (vedas personified as goddesses). This name pictures the dust of Her lotus feet reddening the parting of the personified Vedas' hair like bridal vermilion. The four Vedas as Goddesses bend before Her, and the glow of Her foot-dust marks their crowns, for even crown-texts and Upaniṣads confess inability to define Brahman except by negation and affirmation. The passage adds Saundarya Laharī: Brahmā, Viṣṇu, and Śiva build worlds from that dust, which also scatters seekers' inner dark; earlier names handled ritual portions, this one crowns wisdom. Humility adorns learning. In practice, bow your learning to mystery today, letting one unanswerable question wear Her dust.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-068"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-290", title: "Sakalāgamasandohaśuktisampuṭamauktikā", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who is the pearl enclosed in the shell made of all the scriptures. Sakalāgama-sandoha-śukti-sampuṫa-mauktikā means the pearl in the casket of all scriptural clusters. The passage recalls the nose-ring pearl already praised and widens it: vast traditional sciences of temple, form, stars, and rites are shells around one lustrous truth, since words and thoughts rebound from Brahman unable to grasp it, as Taittirīya says. Beside inward discovery, libraries are small; the pearl outshines its box. Shells of ritual, grammar, and architecture guard a single luster, valuable only while they conduct seekers to the gem. In practice, close the book after study and sit in silence, letting the pearl's luster replace the shell's pattern in your mind.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-068"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-291", title: "Puruṣārthapradā", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who grants the (four-fold) objects of human life. Puruṣārtha-pradā means giver of the four human aims: righteousness, purpose and wealth, pleasure, and liberation. The passage cautions that scriptures do not ban these values but warn against clinging, a point often misquoted into refusal. Another reading hears puruṣa as Śiva, artha as freedom, and pradā as giver: Śiva grants release through Śakti, binding the pair in mutual work. Life's goods become Her gifts. Wealth, pleasure, duty, and freedom each receive Her sanction, provided none is clutched so tightly that it blocks the rest. In practice, pursue one aim in each of the four orders this week, holding each lightly and dedicating its fruit to Her.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-069"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-292", title: "Pūrṇā", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who is always whole, without growth or decay. Pūrṇā means the Full, whole without increase or loss. The passage cites the Bṛhadāraṇyaka peace-chant: that is full, this is full, from fullness fullness proceeds, and subtracting fullness from fullness leaves fullness alone, a peerless portrait of all-pervading Brahman as That. Perfection is not assembled but abiding. Subtracting worlds from wholeness leaves wholeness undiminished, a mathematics by which the heart can divide its love without loss. Hold this fullness against every sense of lack, and watch complaint quietly turn into thanksgiving. In practice, repeat the fullness verse slowly, feeling lack dissolve into a whole that needs no repair.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-069"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-293", title: "Bhoginī", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who is the enjoyer. Bhoginī means enjoyer, She who tastes all luxuries. The passage muses that embodiment of delight savoring its own display follows naturally from fullness proceeding to fullness, creation as Her feast; even the saying that God watches all acts hints at savoring rather than spying. A side-note hears the coiled serpent-power in the word, though out of place at this height. Enjoyment is ownership by love. Guilt poisons pleasure and greed corrupts it, while gratitude restores tasting to its original innocence. In practice, receive one pleasure today gratefully as Her tasting Her own world through you, without guilt or greed.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-069"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-294", title: "Bhuvaneśvarī", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who is the ruler of the universe. Bhuvaneśvarī means Mistress of the worlds, ruler of the fourteen planes above and below earth born of elements and inner faculties. The passage notes Her seed hrīṃ, the allure-syllable blending Śiva, fire, and desire-currents, potent for creation, and observes that while enjoying all She must also govern all, wedding delight to command; Śiva as Bhuvaneśvara shares this reign. Sovereignty savors. Fourteen planes administered as one household prove that vastness need not mean neglect of the smallest room. In practice, order one small domain, a room or schedule, beautifully, rehearsing Her cosmic housekeeping in miniature.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-069"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-295", title: "Ambikā", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who is the mother of the universe. Ambikā means Mother, here distinct from the opening Mother of beings: now parent of the universe itself, living and nonliving together. The passage assigns this motherhood the triple powers of will, knowledge, and action, and recalls the saying that Śiva is day and Śakti night through Her veiling art. To mother the stage as well as the players is ultimate care. Tending things as well as persons widens motherhood beyond sentiment into stewardship of the whole stage. In practice, care for one inanimate thing, a tool, plant, or place, as attentively as for a person, honoring Her wider maternity.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-069"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-296", title: "Anādinidhanā", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who has neither beginning nor end. Anādi-nidhanā means without beginning or end, the mark of Brahman alone. The passage warns of two deceptive elations: mistaking distant glimpse for arrival, and mistaking powers, chance meetings, or sudden flashes for completion, both veils She must lift for the ripe. Eternity also names Her inexhaustible activity, with worship possible through senses, faculties, and mind until dysfunction, complacency, or marvels distract the traveler through many enumerated snares. Endlessness outlasts excitement. Powers, visions, and lucky chances all test humility, since mistaking a milestone for arrival halts the journey. In practice, distrust any thrill claiming final arrival, and return quietly to steady practice.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-069"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-297", title: "Haribrahmendrasevitā", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who is attended by brahmā, viṣṇu and indra. Haribrahmendra-sevitā means served by Viṣṇu, Brahmā, and Indra. The passage underlines Śakti-worship: even creator, preserver, and king of gods bow, and all three are honored within Her diagram-rite. Śiva's absence is explained by identity: as consort He is not other, and static ground cannot function without dynamic power, a truth Vedic hymns approach yet cannot complete without passing beyond mere learning to Mother Herself. The highest serve the Highest. Creator, preserver, and chieftain laying insignia at Her feet confess that delegation returns at last to its fountain. In practice, stop bargaining with intermediate powers and carry your chief petition directly to Her.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-069"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-298", title: "Nārāyaṇī", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who is the female counterpart of nārāyaṇa. Nārāyaṇī is the feminine of Nārāyaṇa, the Abode of Waters and beings. The passage gathers many threads: Śiva and Viṣṇu exchanging halves, the half-female Lord yielding place to the Goddess, and repeated hymn-identifications of Mother with Govinda, Mukunda, and Viṣṇu-forms. Since primal waters first rose from Brahman and then housed it, the dweller therein is Nārāyaṇa, and as nondifferent Brahman She is Nārāyaṇī, a title Viṣṇu's litany also bears in masculine voice. Names converge like rivers. In practice, see Viṣṇu and Devī as one ocean under different moons, and worship without sectarian edge.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-070"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-299", title: "Nādarūpā", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who is in the form of sound. Nāda-rūpā means in the form of resonance, the humming ground of sound. The passage cites Varivasyā Rahasya on the syllable hrīṃ built from breath, fire, glance, drop, crescent, and eight ascending resonances culminating in the subtlest poise, whose totality is called nāda enthroned above the drop. It then traces speech from supreme poise through beholding and middle wordings to uttered voice, changes occurring in the hearer's inner instrument while She Herself remains unmoved; She is word-Brahman. In practice, hum softly and follow the vibration inward from lips to heart to silence, meeting Her as sound's mother.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-070"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-300", title: "Nāmarūpavivarjitā", category: "Tattva", summary: "She who has no name or form. Nāma-rūpa-vivarjitā means free from name and form, devoid of limiting tags. Every creature, the passage explains, joins universal awareness with individual self-sense, the latter flowering into names and shapes while the former abides as being, knowing, and bliss; uniting the small I with Śiva's I mothers the world, but She belongs to the timeless side. Space itself, says Chāndogya, displays names and forms within Brahman while Brahman stays immortal Self, praised here by negation as elsewhere by affirmation. Freedom defines Her. In practice, lay down one label you clutch, even a virtuous one, and rest namelessly in Her for a breath.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-070"] },
];
